  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWarm Friday and Saturday: Confidence is high that above average\ntemps will occur as the flow aloft becomes more \nsoutherly/southwesterly. This is due to a larger upper level \nsystem moving from the West Coast into the Rockies. Latest NBM \ncontinues to support highs in the upper 60s to around 70 both \ndays. The lack of lift and moisture will keep rain chances near\nzero. These high temps will be roughly 10-15 degrees above \naverage. \n\nRain Chances Saturday Night into Sunday: Ensembles continue to \nsuggest that the upper level energy will slide east into \nColorado and eventually Missouri by Sunday. This energy and a \nfront will certainly provide for a lifting mechanism for rain \nhowever the main limiting factor for higher rainfall appears to\nbe moisture quality and depth. That being said, latest NBM now has\na 40-70% chance of rainfall amounts of 0.10in or greater with \nthis system with the highest percentages/amounts north of\nSpringfield. The progressive nature of the front is another \nlimiting factor for higher amounts. Currently low forecast mean\nMU CAPE values are keeping thunderstorm chances less than 20 \npercent. The front/precip moving through on Sunday will usher in\ncooler temperatures. There is still some uncertainty on the \nexact timing of the rainfall Sunday but highest chances are \ncurrently in the morning hours. \n\nCooler air aloft then looks to build in for early next week \nwith temps closer to or slightly below average. Ensemble\nvariance/uncertainty increases with timing and location of \nenergy moving through this colder airmass therefore any precip \nchances remain highly uncertain and currently less than 20 \npercent.",
